LWJGL 3 (org.lwjgl:lwjgl, -glfw, -opengl, -opengles, -egl, -openal, -stb,
-harfbuzz, -tinyfd)
  Copyright Lightweight Java Game Library contributors
  BSD 3-Clause: https://github.com/LWJGL/lwjgl3/blob/master/LICENSE.md
  -opengles is the OpenGL ES the renderer speaks when its context comes from
  ANGLE (below); -egl is classes only, binding the system's libEGL, and brings no
  native library. Beside LWJGL's own JNI code, its natives jars carry native
  libraries under their own licences: libffi (MIT) and, on Linux, liburing (MIT)
  inside its core library; GLFW (zlib); stb (public domain / MIT); HarfBuzz
  (Old MIT); tinyfiledialogs (zlib); and OpenAL Soft, which is not permissive:
  GNU Library General Public License version 2 or, at your option, any later
  version. LWJGL also states the Khronos Group's MIT-style licence for its
  OpenGL, OpenGL ES and EGL modules. None of these texts is inside LWJGL's Maven
  jars: LWJGL keeps each beside its module in its source tree, under
  https://github.com/LWJGL/lwjgl3/tree/master/modules/lwjgl, and records beside
  OpenAL Soft's library in each natives jar the upstream commit it was built from.

  OpenAL Soft is the library the backend plays every sound through, so the
  backend as resolved always contains LGPL code. It is a shared library of its
  own (libopenal.so, libopenal.dylib, OpenAL.dll), opened at run time and never
  linked into Limn's code or LWJGL's, so another build can replace it without
  relinking anything; LWJGL's org.lwjgl.openal.libname property names one.
  Source: https://github.com/kcat/openal-soft

JLayer 1.0.1 (javazoom:jlayer), for MP3 decoding
  Copyright JavaZOOM
  GNU Lesser General Public License 2.1:
  https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.html
  Used unmodified, and never combined with Limn's own code at the source level:
  from Maven Central it is a jar of its own beside limn-backend-lwjgl's, and the
  one bundle this repository builds that contains it — the runnable demo jar
  attached to a GitHub release — carries the same unmodified classes, which
  anyone can replace by re-packing the jar, with the licence text beside them
  at META-INF/licenses/LGPL-2.1.txt.

  It is an ordinary runtime dependency of limn-backend-lwjgl, so an application
  decodes MP3 unless it excludes the jar, and one that excludes it loses MP3
