FFmpeg: libavcodec, libavformat, libavutil, libswresample
  Copyright the FFmpeg developers
  GNU Lesser General Public License 2.1 or later:
  https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.html
  Source: https://ffmpeg.org/releases/
  No FFmpeg binary is in this repository and none is built by it. The libraries,
  and the JNI shim (liblimnffmpeg) in front of them, are the limn-ffmpeg-natives
  artifact, built and released from
  https://github.com/limn-toolkit/limn-ffmpeg-natives on FFmpeg's schedule and
  versioned after it. That repository holds the exact FFmpeg version, its
  checksum and the complete configure line, and the jars it publishes carry the
  licence text and a notice beside the binaries
  (limn/video/ffmpeg/native/LICENSE-ffmpeg.txt and NOTICE-ffmpeg.txt): a jar's
  reader has no repository to look in. limn-video-ffmpeg depends on the shim
  jar; an application adds one natives-<os>-<arch> classifier of the payload,
  or limn-video-ffmpeg-natives-all (published from here) for all six.

  Built WITHOUT --enable-gpl and WITHOUT --enable-nonfree, so no GPL component
  (the three GPL x86 optimisation files, the GPL filters) and no
  nonfree component (Fraunhofer FDK AAC, OpenSSL) is present. x264 and x265 are
  not linked. A test here reads the licence and the configure line back out of
  the linked library, so this paragraph cannot quietly stop being true.

  DYNAMICALLY LINKED. The four FFmpeg libraries are shipped as separate shared
  libraries beside Limn's shim, never statically linked into it, so they can be
  replaced with a modified build without relinking anything, which is the
  freedom the LGPL exists to protect. See
  docs/adr/011-ffmpeg-licence-and-linking.md.

  Without the payload on the classpath the decoder reports itself unavailable
  and nothing else changes. Applications that do not play video can exclude
  limn-video-ffmpeg entirely; it is a separate module and no library published
  from here depends on it (limn-demo, a program, does).

